$DIR/main.p:12:5: cannot send the function literal on ch: the receiver may outlive x
    A function literal that uses variables of the function around it refers to
    those variables where they live, in f's frame (x is declared at main.p:11).
    The value sent here may be received after f returns and x's memory is
    reclaimed.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Pass a value, which is copied, or an owner, which moves, instead of a view
    of the frame.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3005" for a longer explanation)
